releasenotes.d -- RELEASENOTES entries not yet released
=======================================================

A pull request that needs a RELEASENOTES entry adds one file here, and
leaves RELEASENOTES alone. Two pull requests then never write to the same
place, where each adding its entry at the top of RELEASENOTES put every
one of them in conflict with the next one merged.

A file is one entry, exactly as it will read in RELEASENOTES: a title
naming the component, then " -- " and what changes; a line of dashes as
long as the title; an empty line; then prose.

	xymonnet -- what changes for whoever upgrades
	---------------------------------------------

	Who is concerned, what changes for them, what they have to do.

Name it after what it is about, with the suffix .txt: component first,
then a few words, as in xymonnet-tls-servername.txt. The name is not
printed; entries are placed in the order of their names, so those of one
component stay together.

At release, build/releasenotes.sh X.Y.Z moves every entry into the
"Changes for X.Y.Z" section of RELEASENOTES and removes its file
(RELEASING.md, step 1). "build/releasenotes.sh --check" reads the entries
and changes nothing; tests/buildsystem/releasenotes-entries.sh runs it.

What belongs in RELEASENOTES at all, and what an entry says, is in
RELEASING.md, "The changelog". The script refuses a title that is not
"component -- what changes" or is over 80 columns, a line over 80
columns, more than two paragraphs of prose, and a pull request or an
issue named by its number.
